.\" Manual page for scrutiny-agent.conf(5).
.\" Format with:  man ./scrutiny-agent.conf.5  (macOS)   or   man -l scrutiny-agent.conf.5  (Linux)
.TH SCRUTINY-AGENT.CONF 5 "2026-09-21" "scrutiny-agent 0.2.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
scrutiny-agent.conf \- configuration file for scrutiny-agent
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB installDir /scrutiny-agent.conf
.PP
.B scrutiny-agent \-\-rpc\-stdio \-\-config
.I path
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B scrutiny-agent.conf
holds the startup policy of
.BR scrutiny-agent (1):
which directories the agent may read, whether it may write, which git
subcommands
.B git.exec
may run, and where it logs.
It is read when the agent is started with
.BI \-\-config " path"
and has no fixed location; the name above is the convention every
client follows, writing the file beside the agent binary in its
install directory so that every client bootstrapping into the same
directory agrees about what the agent may touch.
.PP
The point of a file rather than an exec line is that policy lives on
the host: it is readable and editable where the agent runs, and it
survives reconnects.
The format is deliberately not JSON.
It is written by hand, or generated to be read by a person deciding
what a machine may touch.
.SH FORMAT
The file is line-oriented.
.IP \(bu 3
Each line is
.BI key " = " value\c
\&.
Whitespace around the key and around the value is trimmed.
The value runs to the end of the line; a value may itself contain
.BR = .
.IP \(bu 3
.B #
starts a comment that runs to the end of the line, wherever it
appears.
A value therefore cannot contain
.BR # .
.IP \(bu 3
Blank lines are ignored.
.IP \(bu 3
A line holding a bare
.I key
with no
.B =
means
.BR "key = true" .
This is meant for the boolean key
.BR allow\-write ;
for any other key the literal value
.B true
is what gets applied.
.IP \(bu 3
Keys are case-insensitive (they are lowercased before use).
Values are used as written, except
.B git\-exec\-config
keys, which are lowercased.
.IP \(bu 3
Repeatable keys
.RB ( allow\-root ,
.BR git\-exec ,
.BR git\-exec\-config ,
.BR git\-exec\-preset ,
.BR config )
accumulate.
Single-valued keys
.RB ( log ,
.BR log\-level ,
.BR allow\-write )
take the last value seen.
.IP \(bu 3
Lines apply in order, and the file applies at the point on the
command line where its
.B \-\-config
appears.
Later command-line options add to or override what the file set, and
earlier ones are added to or overridden by it.
.IP \(bu 3
An unknown key, or a bad value for a known key, is reported on the
agent's standard error with the file name and line number and
otherwise ignored.
An unreadable file is likewise a warning.
One stale line in a shared file must not stop an agent from starting.
.PP
Every key corresponds to the command-line option of the same name
with the leading dashes removed, and the two feed one table inside the
agent, so neither can gain a setting the other lacks.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.BI allow\-root " = path"
Add
.I path
to the filesystem sandbox.
Every path an
.B fs.*
request names must, after symlink resolution, lie inside one of the
allowed roots; anything else is refused with
.B PERMISSION_DENIED
before the kernel is touched.
A relative
.I path
is anchored under the agent's
.BR $HOME .
Repeatable.
A root that does not resolve is dropped with a warning.
If no root survives, the sandbox is
.B $HOME
itself.
.TP
.BR allow\-write " [= true | false]"
Serve the filesystem write methods
.RB ( fs.writeFile ,
.BR fs.mkdir ,
.BR fs.delete ,
.BR fs.rename ,
.BR fs.copy ,
.BR fs.chmod ).
Off by default.
The values
.BR false ,
.B no
and
.B 0
turn it off; anything else, including a bare
.BR allow\-write ,
turns it on.
Writes are confined to the same roots as reads.
.TP
.BI git\-exec " = subcommand"
Allow one git
.I subcommand
through the
.B git.exec
method.
Repeatable.
.B git.exec
is disabled, and not advertised to clients, until at least one
subcommand has been allowed by this key or by
.BR git\-exec\-preset .
Subcommand names are matched exactly.
.TP
.BI git\-exec\-preset " = " read\-only " | " magit
Allow a named set of subcommands.
The two presets are listed under
.B PRESETS
below.
An unknown name is a warning and grants nothing.
Combining a preset with individual
.B git\-exec
lines is fine; the result is the union.
.TP
.BI git\-exec\-config " = key"
Permit
.B git.exec
requests to pass
.BI "\-c " key = value
before the subcommand.
.I key
is either a full
.B section.name
.RB ( diff.context )
or a bare section
.RB ( color ),
which permits every key in that section.
The built-in set is listed under
.B PERMITTED \-c KEYS
below.
Repeatable.
Never add a key that makes git run a program or reach the network on
its own terms:
.BR core.pager ,
.BR core.editor ,
.BR core.sshCommand ,
.BR credential.helper ,
.BR alias.* ,
.BR diff.external ,
.BR *.textconv ,
.BR http.* ,
and the like.
The set is allowlisted, not denylisted, precisely because that family
is too large and too easily extended to enumerate.
.TP
.BI log " = path"
Append the agent's log to
.IR path .
Without this key nothing is logged.
At 8 MiB the file is renamed to
.IB path .1
and a new one started; one backup is kept.
.I path
is used as given (a relative path is relative to the agent's working
directory, which a transport does not guarantee), so name an absolute
path or one the client resolves.
Credential values never appear in the log, at any level, because the
.B logs.tail
method streams the log back to the client.
.TP
.BI log\-level " = " off " | " error " | " warn " | " info " | " debug " | " trace
The threshold for
.BR log .
Defaults to
.B info
when
.B log
is set.
An unrecognised value is treated as
.BR off ,
which disables logging entirely; check the agent's startup line on
standard error if a log stays empty.
At
.B trace
request parameters are logged in full except credentials; below it
content-bearing parameters are redacted.
.TP
.BI config " = path"
Read another file in this format at this point.
A relative
.I path
is anchored under
.BR $HOME .
There is no cycle detection; do not include a file from itself.
.SH PRESETS
.TP
.B read\-only
Subcommands that only inspect a repository:
.PP
.RS
blame, branch, cat-file, check-ignore, check-ref-format, count-objects,
describe, diff, diff-files, diff-index, diff-tree, for-each-ref, grep,
log, ls-files, ls-remote, ls-tree, merge-base, name-rev, rev-list,
rev-parse, shortlog, show, show-ref, status, symbolic-ref, tag, var,
version, whatchanged.
.RE
.IP
Note that
.B branch
and
.B tag
are included: they list by default, but with arguments they create
and delete refs.
An operator who needs strictly read-only behaviour should list
subcommands individually.
.TP
.B magit
Everything in
.B read\-only
plus the working operations magit needs:
.PP
.RS
add, am, apply, bisect, checkout, cherry, cherry-pick, clean, commit,
fetch, format-patch, merge, mv, notes, pull, push, range-diff, rebase,
reflog, remote, reset, restore, revert, rm, stash, submodule, switch,
update-index, worktree.
.RE
.IP
This is a broad grant, close to
.B git
itself, and is documented as such.
.PP
Whatever the allowlist, every
.B git.exec
request is also checked for the git options that would let it run an
arbitrary program or move the repository; see
.BR scrutiny-agent (1),
section
.BR SECURITY .
The subcommands
.BR fetch ,
.BR pull ,
.BR push ,
.BR ls-remote ,
.B clone
and
.B submodule
authenticate through the client-side credential broker; no token is
stored on the host.
.SH PERMITTED \-c KEYS
Always permitted, before any
.B git\-exec\-config
additions.
A bare section name permits every key under it.
.PP
.RS
.nf
advice            (whole section)
color             (whole section)
core.abbrev
core.commitgraph
core.preloadindex
core.quotepath
core.untrackedcache
diff.algorithm
diff.context
diff.mnemonicprefix
diff.noprefix
diff.renames
diff.submodule
gc.auto
i18n.logoutputencoding
log.date
log.showsignature
status.relativepaths
status.showuntrackedfiles
.fi
.RE
.PP
These only affect formatting or local behaviour.
The set exists because magit passes
.B \-c core.preloadIndex=true \-c log.showSignature=false \-c color.ui=false
.B \-c color.diff=false \-c diff.noPrefix=false
on every invocation, so refusing
.B \-c
outright would mean refusing magit.
.SH CLIENTS THAT WRITE THIS FILE
The file is normally generated, and its comments say by whom.
.TP
.B Scrutiny
(Settings > Remote Hosts) renders the host's saved settings into
.IB installDir /scrutiny-agent.conf
on every connection, hash-gated like the binary, and starts the agent
with
.BR "\-\-config " "that file."
It writes
.B allow\-root
lines (the host's Allowed directories), a
.B git\-exec\-preset
line when git commands via the agent are enabled, and
.B log
/
.B log\-level
when logging is on.
The Mac is the source of truth: hand edits to a Scrutiny-managed file
are overwritten at the next connection.
Change the host in Scrutiny instead.
.TP
.B Emacs
.RB ( scrutiny-agent-remote-setup )
writes the same file once and rewrites it only when its own generated
default changes, so hand edits survive.
It may also write
.B allow\-write
(saving through the agent rather than TRAMP) and
.BR "git\-exec\-preset = magit" .
.PP
Scrutiny's reader ignores keys it does not write, matching the agent's
warn-and-continue posture.
A host used from both clients should be configured in Scrutiny, since
its rewrite drops anything the Emacs client added.
.SH EXAMPLES
A host used for code review only, read-only, two source trees, git
inspection allowed:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# scrutiny-agent policy for this host.
allow-root = src            # ~/src
allow-root = /srv/repos
git-exec-preset = read-only
log = /home/me/.scrutiny-agent/agent.log
log-level = warn
.RE
.fi
.PP
A development host driven from Emacs, with saves and magit over the
agent and an extra
.B \-c
key:
.PP
.nf
.RS
allow-root = work
allow-write
git-exec-preset = magit
git-exec-config = merge.conflictstyle
.RE
.fi
.PP
A minimal allowlist instead of a preset:
.PP
.nf
.RS
allow-root = /srv/repos
git-exec = log
git-exec = status
git-exec = diff
git-exec = fetch
.RE
.fi
.PP
Splitting site policy from per-user additions:
.PP
.nf
.RS
config = /etc/scrutiny-agent/site.conf
allow-root = scratch
.RE
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.IB installDir /scrutiny-agent.conf
The conventional location, beside the agent binary.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR scrutiny-agent (1),
.BR git-config (1)
.PP
.I docs/protocol.md
in the source tree describes the
.B git.exec
policy and the
.B fs.*
sandbox normatively.
.PP
Source: https://github.com/lally/scrutiny-agent
